TEXT 248 -232 Left 3 ;#56 Real world capacitor: lead resistance and inductance with a lossy dielectric
TEXT 248 -176 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vi24SpKYYoQ
TEXT 248 -136 Left 2 ;Alan's real world capacitor is Rs and L in series with the capacitance, with Rp across it for\ndielectric leakage. Sweeping it shows the series self-resonance where it stops being a capacitor.
TEXT 800 -16 Left 2 !.ac dec 200 10k 1G
TEXT 800 40 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_MIN MIN mag(V(Z))
TEXT 800 80 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_SRF FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=6.666Meg
TEXT 800 120 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_100K FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=100k
TEXT 800 160 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_100MEG FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=100Meg
TEXT 800 240 Left 2 ;I1 is a 1 A AC probe, so V(Z) reads\ndirectly as the impedance of the whole\nmodel, in ohms.
TEXT 800 352 Left 2 ;C is Alan's "103" marking, 10 nF. L is his\n57 nH figure for 2 inches of 22 AWG lead.\nRs and Rp are illustrative: he draws both\nbut states no value for either.
TEXT 240 504 Left 2 ;Below resonance V(Z) falls like a capacitor, reaches Rs at the series self-resonance near\n6.67 MHz, then rises like an inductor. Above that frequency the part is no longer a capacitor.
